### Sequencing Rationale

**Why Delivery Exceptions First?**
1. **Fastest payback** (6.9 months vs. 17.1 months)
2. **No API blockers** (CRM and driver app APIs available)
3. **Lower technical risk** (no dispatch console dependency)
4. **Higher volume** (180 vs. 90 cases/day)
5. **Builds foundational platform** (CRM integration, HITL governance, monitoring)
6. **Proves agentic value** to skeptical COO (Sarah Whitmore)

**Why Dispatch Adjustments Second?**
1. **Requires Wave 1 platform assets** (CRM integration, HITL workflow, monitoring)
2. **Requires API wrapper build** (£50-80K, 3-6 months) - can be built in parallel with Wave 1
3. **Higher organizational change management** (dispatch team + driver union)
4. **Marginal payback improves to 8.5 months** due to Wave 1 platform reuse
5. **Validates human-led + agent support archetype** (different from Wave 1's agent-led)

**Why Billing Integration Third (Optional)?**
1. **Lowest ROI** (£10-17K annual saving, 21-36 month payback)
2. **Aurum batch lag** (24-48h) limits real-time credit validation
3. **Low volume** (billing-adjacent tasks are subset of Delivery Exceptions)
